// The prop kit — PURE DATA. No THREE.
//
// Team Bondage had six props: snowman, fence post, hay bale, barrel, crate,
// tractor. Six objects across 4096 tiles is a field with some things on it,
// not a working farm in winter, and the three new maps had nothing of their
// own at all. Bryan: "add more props based on the idea of the game being a
// barn, farm, game set in winter with shooter characteristics".
//
// These are built in code rather than as Blender GLBs. The existing six are
// GLBs generated by the art styler and they stay that way; a GLB is the right
// home for a prop that wants a baked texture atlas. But a GLB is also 150
// lines of Python, a Blender run and a binary in the repo per object, and the
// thing this pass needed was TWENTY-SEVEN objects across four maps. Voxel
// boxes in a data file get there, follow the same spec/test pattern as
// viewmodelSpec / hazardSpec / skyBrawlSpec, and cost nothing to load.
//
// Model space: +X right, +Y up, +Z forward. Origin sits on the ground, so
// y=0 is the tile surface the prop stands on.
//
// The bar each prop has to clear (docs/GRAPHICS_QUALITY_LOOP.md, "Minecraft-
// tier"): a recognisable SILHOUETTE and at least TWO material tones. A prop
// that is one flat colour is a coloured box, and the map already has crates.
//
// # PLACEHOLDER ART — procedural, due a hand-drawn pass per DESIGN_PRINCIPLES.

// -- Palette ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Shared with the rest of the game so a new prop can never introduce a
// twenty-eighth brown.
